# Blueprint for MongoDB Collections: raw_threat_feeds, security_bulletins, anomaly_detections with Validation, Indexing, Retention, and Examples

## Executive Summary and Objectives

Security platforms accumulate heterogeneous data from a wide array of sources: machine-curated threat intelligence feeds, authoritative vendor bulletins, and internally generated anomaly events. This blueprint specifies three MongoDB collections—raw_threat_feeds, security_bulletins, and anomaly_detections—designed to store such data in a lossless, query-efficient, and compliant manner. Each collection is defined with explicit JSON Schema validation, carefully chosen indexes, a uniform document envelope, temporal modeling aligned to both ingestion and event occurrence, and retention policies that support auditability while controlling storage costs.

Deliverables include a complete JSON specification for the collections, validation rules, and index definitions to be saved to database/mongodb_collections.json. The design emphasizes extensibility and operational simplicity: a consistent document structure across collections, a minimal set of canonical identifiers, strict type constraints for core fields, and flexible fields for heterogeneous attributes. Indexing focuses on common filters—time, source, severity, tags, and certain enrichment hashes—while text indexes are scoped to avoid unnecessary overhead. A tiered retention approach with configurable policies, TTL (time-to-live) indexes, and archival hooks ensures compliance and cost control. The design also addresses audit requirements, partial unique indexes for idempotent ingestion, and safe rollbacks.

## Requirements, Assumptions, and Constraints

The collections must support several core functions: ingesting unstructured and semi-structured data, correlating bulletins with threat indicators and anomalies, enabling efficient queries by time, source, severity, tags, and content fields, and allowing backfills and upserts from external systems without data loss. Non-functional requirements include predictable query latency, horizontal scalability through sharding, lifecycle management via archival and TTL processes, and comprehensive audit logging across read and write operations. The solution assumes MongoDB as the primary data store with application-level enforcement of validation and idempotency. Constraints include heterogeneous input formats, variable enrichment quality, and cost/performance trade-offs in index selection. Success will be measured by completeness and correctness of validation, high recall/precision for critical queries, low tail latency, storage efficiency, and demonstrable compliance with retention and audit policies.

## Conceptual Data Model

A uniform document envelope is applied to all three collections to standardize how metadata and content are represented and to simplify query and operations patterns. Each document includes a globally unique _id (ObjectId), timestamps for ingestion (ingested_at) and event occurrence (event_time) where applicable, minimal canonical identifiers (feed_id, bulletin_id, anomaly_id), a source block for provenance, severity and confidence scores normalized to a 0–100 scale, and a normalized_tags array for consistent tagging and text search. Indicators and references are modeled as arrays of objects with fields such as type, value, and context, while raw data can be stored either as normalized JSON or as an optional raw snapshot.

Normalization choices follow consistent patterns across collections. Timestamps include at least ingested_at and, for time-based entities like bulletins and anomalies, event_time. Confidence and severity are represented as integers on a 0–100 scale, with severity mapped to standardized levels for readability (info, low, medium, high, critical). The relationship between entities is driven by references and tags: bulletins may reference threat indicators by value and type, while anomalies reference related indicators by indicator_id and may include contributing evidence. Feed-specific or vendor-specific extensions are captured in an extensions map to preserve forward compatibility without constantly altering the core schema.

To provide a concrete foundation for these patterns, Table 1 summarizes the document envelope fields and their definitions, and Table 2 maps canonical identifiers and reference structures across collections.

Table 1: Document Envelope Field Dictionary

| Field                | Type       | Required | Description                                                                                          |
|----------------------|------------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| _id                  | ObjectId   | Yes      | Globally unique identifier for the document.                                                         |
| schema_version       | string     | Yes      | Version of the document schema used.                                                                 |
| created_at           | timestamp  | Yes      | Timestamp when the document was created in the database.                                             |
| updated_at           | timestamp  | Yes      | Timestamp of the last update to the document.                                                        |
| ingested_at          | timestamp  | Yes      | Time the document was ingested by the platform.                                                      |
| event_time           | timestamp  | No       | Time the event described by the document occurred in the real world.                                 |
| feed_id              | string     | No       | Unique identifier for the source feed (raw_threat_feeds).                                            |
| bulletin_id          | string     | No       | Unique identifier for the security bulletin (security_bulletins).                                    |
| anomaly_id           | string     | No       | Unique identifier for the anomaly event (anomaly_detections).                                        |
| source               | object     | Yes      | Provenance metadata including type, name, vendor, source_url, license, and a canonical reference_id. |
| severity             | object     | No       | Normalized severity representation with level and score.                                              |
| confidence           | int        | No       | Confidence score from 0 (none) to 100 (high).                                                        |
| normalized_tags      | array[str] | No       | Curated tags normalized to lowercase and restricted character set.                                   |
| indicators           | array[obj] | No       | Indicator objects with type, value, context, first_seen, last_seen, and sources.                     |
| references           | array[obj] | No       | Reference objects with type (url, advisory, commit), reference_id, title, and publisher.             |
| extensions           | object     | No       | Feed- or vendor-specific extensions captured in a flexible map.                                      |
| enrichment           | object     | No       | Output of enrichment pipelines (e.g., hashes), with flags for availability or completion.            |
| raw                  | object     | No       | Optional raw snapshot of the original document for lossless ingestion.                               |
| history              | array[obj] | No       | Change history with at, field, old_value, new_value, and actor.                                      |
| audit                | object     | No       | Audit metadata including who, when, action, reason, and request_id.                                  |

Table 2: Identifier and Reference Mapping Across Collections

| Collection            | Primary ID         | Related References                                | Indicator Linkage                                         |
|-----------------------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| raw_threat_feeds      | feed_id (string)   | references[].reference_id (e.g., vendor advisory) | indicators[].value/type with context and time bounds      |
| security_bulletins    | bulletin_id (string) | references[].reference_id (URLs, advisories)     | May link to indicators referenced by value/type           |
| anomaly_detections    | anomaly_id (string) | references[].reference_id (tickets, investigations) | indicators[].indicator_id and contributing evidence     |

### Document Envelope

The envelope ensures consistent and auditable records across collections. Timestamps capture both ingestion and event semantics, with event_time serving as the basis for time-window queries and correlations. Canonical IDs facilitate idempotent ingestion and upsert patterns: feed_id for raw feeds, bulletin_id for bulletins, and anomaly_id for anomalies. The source object provides provenance, including type (community, commercial, vendor), name, vendor, source_url, license, and a canonical reference_id for traceability. Indicators are generic objects that allow arbitrary types (e.g., IP, domain, URL, file hash, CVE) and contextual fields, enabling cross-collection joins via value and type without locking the schema to a fixed set. Extensions preserve feed-specific attributes that are not yet standardized; enrichment aggregates post-ingest processing outcomes, such as hashes for deduplication or content-based correlation. The audit and history blocks make change tracking explicit, supporting compliance and operational forensics.

### Canonical Identifiers and References

Stable IDs are mandatory for deduplication and upsert operations. When a source does not provide an inherent identifier, the system composes a deterministic feed_id from source.name, source.type, and a normalized key (e.g., an indicator value or a vendor-defined key). Bulletins and anomalies receive stable IDs from their publishers when available; otherwise, application-level deterministic IDs are derived from normalized titles, vendors, and timestamps. References capture URLs, advisories, commit IDs, or internal ticket identifiers. Indicators link entities across collections by type and value; for example, a bulletin may cite a set of indicators that are also present in raw feeds, enabling correlation with anomalies through tag overlaps and indicator matches.

## Collection Schemas and Validation

Validation is enforced via MongoDB’s JSON Schema mechanisms. The schemas use bsonType and pattern constraints to ensure type correctness, with required fields enforcing the document envelope. The design favors strict typing for timestamps, integers, enums, and arrays, while keeping extensions and enrichment open maps to accommodate variability. Validation rules include structural checks for arrays of objects and conditionally required fields. As part of ongoing evolution, schema_version allows forward-compatible migrations: older documents remain valid, while new fields are introduced with clear defaults and validation semantics.

Table 3 captures common field constraints across collections, and subsequent subsections define the specific schemas.

Table 3: Common Field Constraints Across Collections

| Field                  | Type            | Constraints                                                                                         |
|------------------------|-----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| _id                    | ObjectId        | Generated by database; globally unique.                                                             |
| schema_version         | string          | Any non-empty string; recommend semantic versioning (e.g., “1.0.0”).                                |
| created_at             | timestamp       | bsonType “date”; must be present.                                                                   |
| updated_at             | timestamp       | bsonType “date”; must be present.                                                                   |
| ingested_at            | timestamp       | bsonType “date”; must be present.                                                                   |
| event_time             | timestamp       | bsonType “date”; conditional based on collection semantics.                                         |
| source                 | object          | Required subfields: type (enum), name (string), vendor (string, optional), reference_id (string).   |
| severity.level         | enum            | One of “info”, “low”, “medium”, “high”, “critical”.                                                 |
| severity.score         | int             | 0–100 inclusive when present.                                                                       |
| confidence             | int             | 0–100 inclusive when present.                                                                       |
| normalized_tags        | array[string]   | Elements match pattern “^[a-z0-9_\\-]{1,64}$”; array length ≤ 512.                                  |
| indicators             | array[object]   | Objects contain: type (string), value (string), first_seen/last_seen (timestamp, optional), context (object, optional). |
| references             | array[object]   | Objects contain: type (enum: url, advisory, commit, ticket), reference_id (string), title (string), publisher (string). |
| extensions             | object          | Map of arbitrary keys; values must be serializable.                                                 |
| enrichment             | object          | Flags such as “geolocation_done” (bool), hashes (object with sha256/sha1/md5 as string).            |
| raw                    | object          | Optional original payload snapshot.                                                                 |
| history                | array[object]   | Objects contain at (timestamp), field (string), old_value/new_value (mixed), actor (string).        |
| audit                  | object          | Objects contain who (string), when (timestamp), action (string), reason (string, optional), request_id (string). |

### raw_threat_feeds

The raw_threat_feeds collection stores ingested records from external threat intelligence feeds with lossless fidelity. It supports idempotent ingestion by combining source metadata with deterministic keys. Indicators are modeled generically to accommodate IPs, domains, URLs, hashes, and other types. Enrichment hashes facilitate deduplication and correlation.

Schema highlights:

- Required fields: _id, schema_version, created_at, updated_at, ingested_at, source, severity (optional), confidence (optional), normalized_tags (optional), indicators (optional), references (optional), extensions (optional), enrichment (optional).
- Types: timestamps as bsonType “date”; severity as object with level (enum) and score (int 0–100); confidence as int 0–100; normalized_tags as array of strings constrained by pattern; indicators and references as arrays of objects with defined subfields; enrichment flags and hashes in an object.
- Extensions: a flexible map for feed-specific details.
- Raw snapshot: optional lossless original payload.

Table 4: raw_threat_feeds Fields and Constraints

| Field/Subfield                  | Type            | Required | Validation Notes                                                                                              |
|---------------------------------|-----------------|----------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| schema_version                  | string          | Yes      | Any non-empty string.                                                                                         |
| created_at, updated_at          | timestamp       | Yes      | bsonType “date”.                                                                                              |
| ingested_at                     | timestamp       | Yes      | bsonType “date”.                                                                                              |
| feed_id                         | string          | No       | If present, used for idempotency; stable and unique per feed source.                                          |
| source.type                     | enum            | Yes      | “community”, “commercial”, “vendor”.                                                                          |
| source.name                     | string          | Yes      | Non-empty.                                                                                                    |
| source.vendor                   | string          | No       | Optional.                                                                                                     |
| source.source_url               | string          | No       | Optional URL string.                                                                                          |
| source.license                  | string          | No       | Optional license text.                                                                                        |
| source.reference_id             | string          | Yes      | Stable reference ID for traceability.                                                                         |
| severity.level                  | enum            | No       | “info”, “low”, “medium”, “high”, “critical”.                                                                  |
| severity.score                  | int             | No       | 0–100 inclusive if present.                                                                                   |
| confidence                      | int             | No       | 0–100 inclusive if present.                                                                                   |
| normalized_tags[]               | string          | No       | Each matches pattern “^[a-z0-9_\\-]{1,64}$”; array length ≤ 512.                                              |
| indicators[].type               | string          | No       | Indicator type (e.g., “ip”, “domain”, “url”, “hash”, “cve”).                                                  |
| indicators[].value              | string          | No       | Indicator value; length ≤ 2048.                                                                               |
| indicators[].context            | object          | No       | Optional contextual metadata.                                                                                 |
| indicators[].first_seen         | timestamp       | No       | bsonType “date”.                                                                                              |
| indicators[].last_seen          | timestamp       | No       | bsonType “date”.                                                                                              |
| references[].type               | enum            | No       | “url”, “advisory”, “commit”, “ticket”.                                                                        |
| references[].reference_id       | string          | No       | Stable identifier or URL.                                                                                     |
| references[].title              | string          | No       | Optional title.                                                                                               |
| references[].publisher          | string          | No       | Optional publisher name.                                                                                      |
| extensions                      | object          | No       | Flexible map; values must be serializable.                                                                    |
| enrichment.flags.geolocation_done | bool          | No       | Boolean flag.                                                                                                 |
| enrichment.hashes.sha256        | string          | No       | Hex-encoded SHA-256; length 64.                                                                               |
| enrichment.hashes.sha1          | string          | No       | Hex-encoded SHA-1; length 40.                                                                                 |
| enrichment.hashes.md5           | string          | No       | Hex-encoded MD5; length 32.                                                                                   |
| raw                             | object          | No       | Optional snapshot of the original payload.                                                                    |
| history                         | array[object]   | No       | Change entries with at, field, old_value, new_value, actor.                                                   |
| audit                           | object          | No       | Audit metadata with who, when, action, reason, request_id.                                                    |

Example document:

```json
{
  "_id": { "$oid": "6700f1b3a1d2c34f7bd08abc" },
  "schema_version": "1.0.0",
  "created_at": { "$date": "2025-10-30T01:00:00Z" },
  "updated_at": { "$date": "2025-10-30T01:00:00Z" },
  "ingested_at": { "$date": "2025-10-30T00:59:00Z" },
