## Indexing Strategy and Query Patterns

Indexes are chosen to optimize common queries while minimizing write overhead. Compound indexes align to filters used together—time windows with source or severity; status transitions with time for anomalies; tag-based searches for targeted triage; and lookup paths for enrichment and references. Text indexes are included but restricted to fields that benefit full-text search, such as titles and summaries for bulletins. Partial indexes narrow the indexed subset to active or high-value records, reducing storage and improving write performance. Idempotency is supported with partial unique indexes on canonical IDs.

Table 7: Index Definitions Overview

| Collection             | Index Key(s)                                              | Options                                 | Purpose                                                                                  |
|------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| raw_threat_feeds       | ingested_at:1, source.name:1, severity.score:1           | N/A                                      | Fast filters by ingestion time, source, severity.                                        |
| raw_threat_feeds       | normalized_tags:1                                         | N/A                                      | Tag-based filtering.                                                                     |
| raw_threat_feeds       | indicators.value:1, indicators.type:1                     | N/A                                      | Indicator lookup by value/type.                                                          |
| raw_threat_feeds       | enrichment.hashes.sha256:1                                | Partial index where enrichment.hashes exists | Deduplication via SHA-256.                                                              |
| raw_threat_feeds       | feed_id:1                                                 | Partial unique where feed_id exists      | Idempotent ingestion.                                                                    |
| raw_threat_feeds       | $**:**text on title and references[].title                | Weights: title=5, references.title=1     | Full-text search on feed record titles and referenced titles.                            |
| security_bulletins     | published_at:1, updated_at:1, severity.score:1            | N/A                                      | Time-based queries and severity filters.                                                 |
| security_bulletins     | cve_ids:1                                                 | N/A                                      | Rapid CVE lookup.                                                                        |
| security_bulletins     | affected_products:1                                       | N/A                                      | Product-driven queries.                                                                  |
| security_bulletins     | references.reference_id:1                                 | N/A                                      | Quick reference-based retrieval.                                                         |
| security_bulletins     | bulletin_id:1                                             | Partial unique where bulletin_id exists  | Idempotent upsert.                                                                       |
| security_bulletins     | $**:**text on title, summary                               | Weights: title=5, summary=1              | Full-text search.                                                                        |
| anomaly_detections     | event_time:1, status:1, severity.score:1                  | Compound for triage views                | Time-window filters with status and severity.                                            |
| anomaly_detections     | entities.id:1, entities.type:1                            | N/A                                      | Entity-based investigations.                                                             |
| anomaly_detections     | related_indicators.value:1, related_indicators.type:1     | N/A                                      | Indicator linkage for correlation.                                                       |
| anomaly_detections     | references.reference_id:1                                 | N/A                                      | Investigation ticket lookup.                                                             |
| anomaly_detections     | anomaly_id:1                                              | Partial unique where anomaly_id exists   | Idempotent upsert.                                                                       |
| anomaly_detections     | status:1, updated_at:1                                    | Partial index where status in open set   | Operational queues for triage and investigation.                                         |

### Query Patterns per Collection

Common queries for raw_threat_feeds include time-window retrieval by ingested_at, filtering by source.name and severity.score, tag-based searches using normalized_tags, and indicator lookups by indicators.value and indicators.type. Updates frequently append new indicators, adjust severity or confidence, and set enrichment flags or hashes; idempotent updates rely on feed_id. Security bulletins often filter by published_at and updated_at ranges, severity score, CVEs in cve_ids, affected_products containment, and perform full-text search on title and summary. Updates reflect patch status changes and enriched metadata. Anomaly_detections are typically queried by event_time ranges, status transitions, severity score thresholds, entity filters by entities.id and entities.type, and linked indicators via related_indicators.value and related_indicators.type. Updates adjust status, assignee fields (if introduced later), and close out records upon resolution.

## Data Lifecycle and Retention Policies

Lifecycle management balances compliance, auditability, and cost. Raw feeds are retained for a short to medium window, for example 90 days in the hot tier and up to 12 months in warm/cold tiers, subject to regulatory requirements. Bulletins have longer retention given their authoritative nature; hot retention could be 365 days with warm/cold extension as needed. Anomaly detections sit in the middle, with operational retention of 180 days in the hot tier, extended in warm/cold tiers depending on investigation and audit mandates. Archival involves exporting documents to cold storage or an archive collection with minimal indexing and constrained access. TTL indexes are applied to derivative collections or to documents flagged for deletion when retention expires and no legal hold applies.

Table 8: Retention Matrix

| Collection            | Hot Tier (Indexed)                               | Warm Tier (Sparse Indexed)                               | Cold Tier (Archived/Constrained Access)                  |
|-----------------------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| raw_threat_feeds      | Retain indexed for ~90 days; support TTL deletion | Move to warm after ~90 days; retain up to ~12 months      | Archive beyond ~12 months; minimal indexes               |
| security_bulletins    | Retain indexed for ~365 days                      | Warm tier up to ~3 years                                  | Archive beyond ~3 years; long-term compliance            |
| anomaly_detections    | Retain indexed for ~180 days                      | Warm tier up to ~1 year                                   | Archive beyond ~1 year; restricted access                |

Archival operations copy documents to archive collections or object storage and drop or anonymize fields in the hot tier as required by policy. Deletion workflows honor legal holds: when a document is under hold, TTL-based deletion is suppressed until the hold is cleared. Periodic audits confirm adherence to the schedule and document exceptions.

## Operational Policies: Ingestion, Deduplication, and Idempotency

Ingestion pipelines implement validation at the edge, followed by database-side validation enforcement. Idempotent upserts rely on canonical identifiers: feed_id for raw feeds, bulletin_id for bulletins, and anomaly_id for anomalies. When natural IDs are missing, deterministic IDs are derived from normalized source, content hash, and key attributes such as titles or indicator values. Partial unique indexes enforce uniqueness only when the relevant ID is present. Deduplication leverages content hashing (e.g., enrichment.hashes.sha256) and composite keys of source and normalized fields. Staging and backfill procedures isolate bulk loads, validate, and apply compaction jobs that merge extensions or remove redundant fields. Migration strategies use schema_version to introduce changes while preserving compatibility with historical documents.

Table 9: Idempotency Keys and Partial Unique Index Definitions

| Collection            | Partial Unique Index Key(s)         | Filter Condition                                 | Purpose                                |
|-----------------------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| raw_threat_feeds      | feed_id:1                            | feed_id exists                                   | Prevent duplicate feed records.        |
| raw_threat_feeds      | enrichment.hashes.sha256:1           | enrichment.hashes.sha256 exists                  | Deduplicate by content hash.           |
| security_bulletins    | bulletin_id:1                        | bulletin_id exists                               | Ensure unique vendor bulletins.        |
| anomaly_detections    | anomaly_id:1                         | anomaly_id exists                                | Idempotent anomaly upserts.            |

## Security, Compliance, and Audit

Data protection requires encryption at rest and in transit, role-based access control with least privilege, and field-level controls for sensitive elements. The audit block captures who performed an action, when, what changed, and why, while the history block records field-level changes. Redaction strategies target sensitive identifiers in extensions and evidence, and hashing or tokenization is applied where appropriate. Compliance measures include watermarking via schema_version, explicit retention schedules, legal holds, and data subject request handling in extensions or dedicated metadata collections. Query logging and rate limiting guard against data exfiltration and abuse.

Table 10: Audit Fields and Change History Schema

| Block    | Fields                                   | Description                                                       |
|----------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| audit    | who (string), when (timestamp), action (string), reason (string, optional), request_id (string) | Records operational actions and provenance.                       |
| history  | at (timestamp), field (string), old_value (mixed), new_value (mixed), actor (string) | Captures field-level changes over time for traceability.          |

